---
title: "Tiny Satellites, Big Questions About Urban Heat"
summary: "A made-up research team tests pocket-sized satellites in a story about how technology could reshape climate planning."
category: Technology
date: 2026-11-05
author: "Ari Morgan"
---

At the fictional Northbridge Institute, researchers are imagining a fleet of tiny satellites that could help cities understand where summer heat builds up.

Their concept, called PocketSky, pairs compact orbiting sensors with neighborhood weather stations to create detailed heat maps.

## Seeing the city differently

In this fictional scenario, planners compare tree-covered streets with large paved areas. They use the maps to decide where shade structures, green spaces, or reflective surfaces might help.

The scientists emphasize that data alone cannot decide which neighborhoods need investment. Community priorities would have to guide every decision.

## An idea, not a real mission

PocketSky and the Northbridge Institute are invented. No satellite launch or scientific result is being reported here.

This sample article illustrates how a technology story can explain an emerging idea while clearly separating possibility from established fact.
